Table~\ref{tab:conv} shows that the restriction leaves three of the four
solvers unchanged in every respect that matters: ABCE3, fEABCLS and TSN2
keep the same best arm, the same ordering and the same qualitative
conclusion. The absolute levels rise by one to three tenths of a point
because the cells that converge are the easier ones.

The GA is the exception, and the difference is not cosmetic. Over all 61
instances the unseeded control is the best arm (7.52 against MIX's 7.58);
over the 11 instances where that control actually converges, the ordering
reverses and the two best seeded arms beat it by 0.39 points (5.00 against
5.39). The negative result for the GA is therefore a statement about
\emph{insufficient budget}, not about seeding as such: it is the solver
that needs the most time to consolidate a seeded population, and on the
cells where it is given that time the harm disappears. We carry this
qualification through the rest of the paper, and it revises the reading of
Section~\ref{sec:whyga}: what the GA lacks is not compatibility with
heterogeneous parents in principle, but enough generations to exploit them
under these budgets.

Unless stated otherwise, the tables that follow use all 61 instances, so
that every solver is evaluated on the same instance set; results
restricted to converged cells are reported wherever they change a
conclusion, which is the GA alone.

